## API keys (`ak`)

Approved low-risk secrets are GPG-encrypted in the WSL distro explicitly named
by `~/.config/ak/vault.conf` (currently `Debian-Recovered`, but managed
configuration may change it). Never use the default WSL distro implicitly,
search other distros, fall back to Windows Credential Manager, or automate
Bitwarden access.

On Windows, use the native PowerShell 7 entrypoint. It routes only to the configured named distro and fails closed when it is unavailable:

```powershell
# List service names only; this does not retrieve values.
& {{ $windowsEnv }}/Get-AkSecret.ps1 -List
```
Prefer the consuming skill's managed launcher, which captures credentials internally. If manual loading is necessary, load `windows-env` and capture the approved helper's value inside the consuming command, without printing it. From Bash the helper is `bash {{ $windowsEnv }}/ak-get <SERVICE>`; from PowerShell 7 it is `Get-AkSecret.ps1 -Service <SERVICE>`. Never invoke value-returning helpers alone in an agent tool call.
The managed `~/.envrc`
is the reviewed export profile; import only its explicit service allowlist. Do
not use no-argument bulk loading or add `export: false` credentials. Never store
keys in plaintext dotfiles, and retrieve them only when explicitly required.

